  "transcript": "# FCDO's approach to higher education and research partnerships\n\n## Who was speaking and why it matters\n> IDS and Universities UK International described universities as key development infrastructure: they teach, build local research capacity and create long-lasting diplomatic ties through scholarships and partnerships.\n\nChair: We are looking at higher education and research partnerships the UK supports; please introduce yourselves briefly and say what your organisations do internationally.\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: I run the Institute of Development Studies (IDS) at Sussex: we’re a research institute with masters programmes, focused on applied development problems and training people who go on to work in policy and practice in the global south.\nHarry Anderson: I’m deputy director at Universities UK International, which represents 142 UK universities; we push that universities are critical development infrastructure—they teach, research and build long-term partnerships abroad.\nChair: So you both see universities as not just commercial exporters but institutions that strengthen countries and create ties important to UK foreign policy.\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Exactly—students and researchers trained in the UK or on UK programmes often stay in-country but use UK methods and networks, and those relationships matter when those people later hold influence.\n\n\n## Scholarships, visas and soft power\n> Commonwealth and Chevening scholarships are high-value soft power tools, but recent cuts and visa restrictions are damaging UK reputation and blocking talented students from coming.\n\nDr Sam Rushworth: You’ve seen reductions in some scholarship programmes. What does that look like on the ground for research and country influence?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Cuts and visa brakes have immediate effects: IDS went from 24 funded scholars in 2025–26 to seven this year because some countries were blocked, and that undermines our teaching and networks.\nDavid Mundell: Do you have evidence that scholarship alumni stay and contribute back home, or do lots leave for better jobs elsewhere?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Many alumni are motivated to return; institutions track this and embassies follow up. We can provide reports showing alumni impact—people who go on to influential public or private roles.\nChair: On visa policy: could some students—Chevening or Commonwealth scholars—have been exempted? Was the blanket approach necessary?\nHarry Anderson: The visa brake damaged perceptions of the UK as a reliable partner. A targeted exemption for vetted scholarship recipients would have preserved commitments and saved reputational damage.\nMonica Harding: Where else will students go if they can’t come to the UK?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Some will take offers in Europe, Australia or other English-speaking options—but language, quality and fit vary; losing the UK option is a big loss for many.\n\n\n## What good ODA-funded research partnerships do\n> Long-term, locally owned ODA-funded partnerships build trust, train researchers in-country, and produce practical policy changes that short-term projects or private investors usually can't achieve.\n\nJames Naish: With GCRF and SPHEIR gone or cut, what is lost? How should successors be designed?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: GCRF and earlier programmes funded networks, early-stage translational research, and training. A successor needs sustained funding, local ownership, and linkages between research and teaching.\nHarry Anderson: Universities want a successor with a multi-year funding envelope, clear priorities and enough time to rebuild trust with partners, otherwise short projects just won’t deliver systemic change.\nDr Sam Rushworth: Are there low-cost models to build research capacity?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Yes: IDS’s multi-methods course trained thousands of African mid-career researchers; initial external funding seeded a programme that local cohorts then sustained—replicable and high impact.\nChair: So the focus should be on localisation: local researchers leading agendas and long-term mentoring, not brief UK-led projects.\n\n\n## Private finance and transnational education\n> Private capital and transnational education (TNE) can help but won’t replace public ODA; TNE offers a major opportunity to scale education in-country, provided quality and local development goals are central.\n\nTracy Gilbert: The Government looks to private investment to fill ODA gaps—what are the risks for development research?\nHarry Anderson: Private capital can catalyse projects and scale innovations, but it tends to chase monetisable returns and specific geographies; public funding must remain for public goods and early-stage risk-taking.\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Agreed—private finance won’t fund many public goods, like certain health services or orphan crops; regulation and public oversight are essential to avoid inequitable outcomes.\nJames Naish: How should the UK use the large TNE footprint—hundreds of thousands studying for UK awards overseas—as part of development policy?\nHarry Anderson: TNE is now a major channel: almost 700,000 students study for UK degrees overseas. If aligned to local skills needs and quality assured, TNE can build capacity in-country without driving brain drain.\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: Linking TNE to research partnerships gives a two-way benefit: students learn from research and bring it into their countries, while research gains local insight and continuity.\n\n\n## Security, university finances and practical asks of government\n> Universities face genuine research-security risks and financial pressures; they want clearer, long-term government engagement, smart visa exemptions, and protection for vulnerable students and academic freedoms.\n\nJames Naish: Universities have faced warnings about foreign influence. How are you managing research security and partnerships—do we need stricter controls?\nHarry Anderson: Universities take security seriously: we’ve strengthened due diligence, supported the Research Collaboration Advice Panel and an academic-interference reporting route; but we must balance openness, research freedom and safety.\nChair: Do universities have the capacity to spot covert risks, and are you asking for more government support?\nHarry Anderson: We’ve made progress—ATAS expanded, MI5 has engaged vice-chancellors—but universities need resources and coherent government channels to coordinate security, export control and diplomacy.\nDavid Mundell: What about financial strain on universities and the knock-on effect on international partnerships?\nHarry Anderson: The sector is under acute financial pressure; international student income now underpins many institutions. That fragility reduces universities’ ability to sustain overseas partnerships—longer-term public funding would help.\nChair: Final quick asks: one line each—are your voices heard in government and what do you need most?\nProfessor Anuradha Joshi: We have influence but need clearer, long-term funding streams and better recognition of universities as development infrastructure.\nHarry Anderson: We want joined-up government engagement, visa flexibility for vetted scholars, and sustained multi-year funding to rebuild trust with partners.\n",
